<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1">
  <DUT Name="ST_BP_ArbInternal" Id="{cafc2631-22b5-4976-a8a9-7355cd6daf26}">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(* ST_BP_ArbInternal - one row of an arbiter's beam parameter assertion pool.
A. Wallace 2020-08-07

Purpose:
    Pair a beam parameter set (the inherited ST_BeamParams members) with the
    identity of the request that submitted it, so the arbiter's assertion pool
    can report who is asserting what. FB_Arbiter.AddRequest stores one of these
    values per request in FB_BeamParamAssertionPool, keyed by the request ID.
    The three members declared below are that bookkeeping; they are not beam
    parameters.

Usage:
    Internal to the PMPS library - device, IOC and application code uses
    ST_BeamParams, not this type. AddRequest is the only writer: it MEMCPYs the
    caller's ST_BeamParams over a local instance of this type (sound because
    this type EXTENDS ST_BeamParams), then sets nId, LiveInTable and sDevName.
    The pytmc pragmas below export those three members to EPICS as read-only
    diagnostics of the pool; ST_BeamParams' own pragmas export the parameters.

Objectives:
    - One pool lookup returns both the beam parameters to arbitrate and the
      identity needed to attribute them.
    - The exported assertion-pool table shows, per row, which request holds the
      row (nId), whether the row is still claimed (LiveInTable) and by which
      device (sDevName), with no second table to keep in step.
    - Identify rows only. The arbitration math (FB_Arbiter.ArbitrateBP) reads
      and writes the inherited ST_BeamParams members and never looks at these.

Caution: AddRequest and FB_BeamParamAssertionPool.DataPoolToEpics copy whole
structs with MEMCPY, and the exported EPICS Entry array follows this layout. Do
not reorder, resize or insert members here or in ST_BeamParams without
regenerating PMPS.tmc and the pytmc-generated IOC records.

History:
    2020-08-07 A. Wallace - type created with nId and LiveInTable to pack the
        request ID into the pool (7ac36a2, "An attempt at packing the ID into
        the arbiter pool so we can figure out where requests come from").
    2020-08-20 A. Wallace - pytmc pragmas added for the arbiter diagnostics
        (acfeeba, "PVs for better arbiter diags.").
    2021-05-05 ghalym - sDevName added with its pytmc pragma (4d6c033, "DEV:
        Adding sDev name to address issue#82").
    2026-11-27 - header and per-member comments added; no member, type or
        layout change (POU header comment audit, task #294).
*)
TYPE ST_BP_ArbInternal EXTENDS ST_BeamParams :
STRUCT
    // Arbitration request ID: the nReqID the device passed to AddRequest
    // (unique per device + state combination), which is also this row's hash
    // table key. Unitless 32-bit identifier, not an array index. 0 is never a
    // live request (AddRequest rejects it) and is what RemoveRequest writes
    // into a row that is being retired.
    {attribute 'pytmc' := 'pv: ID
        io: i
    '}
    nId : DWORD;
    // TRUE while this row holds a live, asserted request; set by AddRequest
    // and never cleared by arbitration itself. FALSE marks a retired row:
    // RemoveRequest overwrites the row with a zeroed filler before deleting it
    // so the exported table never shows stale data. Diagnostics only.
    {attribute 'pytmc' := 'pv: Live
        io: i
    '}
    LiveInTable : BOOL;
    // Report label naming the requester: the sDevName given to AddRequest, or
    // "Arbiter ID: 0x...." when a lower authority arbiter elevates its own
    // request. Free text for the EPICS table, not a PLC path or PV name; held
    // as STRING(80), so a longer caller-supplied name is truncated.
    {attribute 'pytmc' := 'pv: Device
        io: i
    '}
    sDevName: STRING;
END_STRUCT
END_TYPE
]]></Declaration>
  </DUT>
</TcPlcObject>